#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "0350faac-e5c6-56dc-af12-3c90e0c16bf4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5378" :question/problem "Given that the asymptotes of the hyperbola below are $y=\\pm\\dfrac{5}{7}x$, find the parametric equations of the hyperbola.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/873/Source/Images/6dac6525092db5cbe84cc8ace803557c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a, 0)$. From the graph, we have that $(7,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7$.\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm\\dfrac57x\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{b}{a} &= \\frac{5}{7} \\\\\n\\frac{b}{7} &= \\frac{5}{7} \\\\\nb &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=5\\tan{t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "961ff66e-ce82-5ef7-bd5e-d5aa14c1ec58" :question/math-academy-id "e-5379" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola with the parametric equations $x = 6\\sec{t},\\, y = 4 \\sqrt{2}\\tan{t}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t$. We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 6\\sec t \\\\\n\\frac{x}{6} &= \\sec t \\\\\n(\\frac{x}{6})^{2} &= (\\sec t)^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{6^{2}} &= \\sec^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{36} &= \\sec^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4\\sqrt{2}\\tan t \\\\\n\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} &= \\tan t \\\\\n(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}})^{2} &= (\\tan t)^{2} \\\\\n(y^{2})/((4\\sqrt{2})^{2}) &= \\tan^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{32} &= \\tan^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}\n$$\n\nand rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} t + 1 &= \\sec^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{32} + 1 &= \\frac{x^{2}}{36} \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{36} - \\frac{y^{2}}{32} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2669e657-da47-5e22-9ac5-7a69f3b0a55f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/id #uuid "0fc71252-9b97-560d-be87-74b36d515823" :question/math-academy-id "e-7883" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\displaystyle\\frac{x^2}{49} - \\dfrac{y^2}{9} = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{49} = 7\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nb =\\sqrt9= 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=3\\tan{t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "5c66fba2-b645-543a-8417-bbc246db55e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7896" :question/problem "The quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/873/Source/Images/c60e96f8795a9be0304c10941f61c928.png)\n\n$x = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad t \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad t \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/873/Source/Images/446c464de9a66d886281a25f332bc654.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[MATH: \\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$[MATH: x= \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad t \\in \\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right).]$"}]}
